Skill levels for dagger determine how many you throw
in a single turn.  This makes thrown daggers extremely
valuable.  Either a hit from throwing or a hit from
melee both count for #enhance-ment as far as I know
but I have not tried to do an exact count.

> > Once you've advanced both long sword and dagger to Skilled
> > try putting away your shield and practicing with #twoweapon.
>
> I'd say you would rather find yourself another longsword-class
> weapon, if you want to #twoweapon.

Until you have a second sword class weapon it makes sense
to use your initial dagger some, at least against small
monsters.  From the first time you pick up a second
dagger-class weapon until you ponder the planes, it makes
sense to throw daggers.

So in my opinion Mjollnir as an excellent early game
weapon, an okay middle game weapon and a poor end game
weapon.  The initial dagger is good enough for melee in
some early game situations and having a second sword
class is better middle or late game.  In fact late game
I prefer a silver saber as the second weapon.

> > Once you're Skilled at that you'll be a killing machine.
>
> Yes, #twoweaponing is effective, but not being able to use a shield
> hinders your AC... Especially for an early-game Valk, since they
> have that +3 small shield.

Given the number of XP slots it takes to get to Skilled
in sword plus dagger plus #twoweapon, the power of
#twoweapon starts to appear in the middle game around
the time when your other armor makes up for the lack of
a shield.  So I agree with your assessment that the
shield matters in the early game not not later.

> > Missiles.  Daggers are for throwing in addition to wielding.
>
> I admit, this might be a reason to advance the dagger-skill, but I
> don't advance it myself. Perhaps it's the reason I've never ascended
> :-)

Could be.  Kill a dangerous monster before it gets to you
and your chances of being alive to face the next one is
higher.  And since Basic throws 1 dagger in 1 turn,
Skilled throws 2 daggers in 1 turn, Expert throws 3 daggers
in 1 turn, the usefullness of daggers just keeps going up.
Then factor in the fact that the enchantment level of your
primary stack of daggers goes up over time and it takes a
huge difference.

Before finishing the mines a Valk might have 1-3 regular
daggers, 5-8 orcish daggers and 1-2 eleven daggers all
+0 and at Basic skill.  They are unimpressive weapons
but they can still reduce the size of a crowd of gnomes or
orcs.  By a few levels past Sokobon plus the mines a Valk
may have built up a stack of a dozen elven daggers, gotten
them up to +3 and advanced to Skilled.  They they are able
to take out many monsters weaker than trolls.  By the time
a Valk has completed the Quest and is looking at the Castle
gates, she could be Expert in dagger, have a stack of at
least a dozen elven daggers, and have them at +6.  They
are able to take out soldiers coming across the drawbridge.
Sometimes throwing 3 in the same turn one toss might take
out a soldier plus something behind the soldier.

Daggers rule.

Other advantages: The occasional silver dagger is even
better.  If you manage to get an athame you have a magical
engraving tool that doesn't dull as long as you keep it
uncursed.  That's so valuable it can be worth a spare
wish just before taking out the Wiz for the first time.

And for classes that aren't combat wombats (priests,
wizards and so on) daggers shine even more in comparison
to other weapon classes.

Some classes can advance both knife and dagger.  There
are no artifact knives but crysknives are in the knife
class.  An artifact-less ascension might want to advance
knife but that's about it.  There are 3 artifact daggers
that I can think of off the top of my head: Magicbane,
Grimtooth, Sting.  Not the most impressive weapons in
the game but artifacts still beat non-artifacts in many
cases.


